an array parameter in a K&R definition is rejected
==================================================

A parameter whose declared type is an array is refused when the
definition is written K&R style.  The same type in an ANSI prototype is
accepted.

	foo(a) int a[];    { return a[0]; }	BAD
	foo(a) char a[];   { return a[0]; }	BAD
	foo(a) char *a[];  { return **a; }	BAD
	foo(a) int *a[];   { return **a; }	BAD
	foo(a) char **a;   { return **a; }	ok

	int foo(int a[])   { return a[0]; }	ok
	int foo(char *a[]) { return **a; }	ok

Four diagnostics come out, all pointing at the line holding the opening
brace of the body:

	x.c:1: fn array
	x.c:1: fn array
	x.c:1: redecl disagrees
	x.c:1: bad decl token

and c0 exits 1 without writing an object.

Two things it is NOT.  It is not about main - plain foo fails
identically, and main was only how it was noticed.  It is not about the
star - a bare "int a[]" fails the same way, and "char **a" passes.  It
is the array, and only in the K&R parameter declaration.

The parameter should decay to a pointer.  "int a[]" as a parameter means
"int *a", so the two rows that pass and the four that fail are the same
four types written two ways, and only one spelling works.


where it is
-----------

Not established, but the search is narrow.  declare.c says c0 does not
handle K&R at all:

	src/pass1/declare.c:560
	 *   - ANSI style only (K&R style is converted by cpp preprocessor)

and the conversion is src/cpp/knr.c.  That file already carries a
per-parameter `post` array for declarator suffixes and emits it after
the parameter name, so the machinery for `[]` exists.  It also has an
abort path - abort_knr() - that gives up and re-emits the original K&R
tokens when the input "stops looking like K&R", and c0, being ANSI
only, would then meet raw K&R text and complain.

That fits the evidence.  If knr.c normalized these correctly the output
would be exactly the ANSI form in the table above, which c0 compiles
without a word.  It does not, so either knr.c aborts on the array
suffix or emits something that is not quite the prototype.  Dumping the
normalizer's output for a failing case decides which; -E and -p on cpp
did not produce a readable .i here, so that was left undone.

"fn array" itself is ER_D_FA, raised in nine places in declare.c.  The
message means a function returning an array, which is a real error and
the right complaint about "foo()[]" - so the diagnostic is probably
honest about what c0 was handed, and the fault is upstream of it.


how it was found
----------------

Porting FORMATMW.C, Morrow's 1982 hard disc formatter, off the
Whitesmiths library so it could be built with ccc -m cpm and run
against the hddma simulation.  Its entry point is ordinary period C:

	main (ac, av)
		int	ac;
		char	*av[];
		{

Whitesmiths compiled that in 1981 without comment.  The whole 1595 line
file went through - every function in the compatibility block, then
usage, error, fmt, builds - and main was the one that did not parse,
which is what made it look at first like something about main.

That is the reason this matters beyond one file.  Any source of that
vintage declares argv this way, and a good deal of it declares other
array parameters too, so this will be met again on the next old program
brought up rather than being a one-off.

Workaround until it is fixed: spell the parameter as a pointer.

	main (ac, av)
		int	ac;
		char	**av;
		{


and then c1 segfaults
---------------------

A separate fault, behind the first one and only reachable once the
parameter is spelled as a pointer.  Not investigated - recorded here
because this is where it was met.

	src/micronix/stand/formatmw.c

cpp and c0 both succeed on that file.  c1 dies:

	cpp -i/usr/local/lib/include -DCCC -o t formatmw.c	rc=0
	c0 t.x t.1 t.2						rc=0
	c1 t.1 t.2 t.s						SIGSEGV

It gets a long way in first - 95733 bytes of assembler written before
it goes.  The last symbol emitted is _report, and the file ends part
way through an expression just after a `call _putfmt`, so report() or
whatever c1 reached next is the place to look.  report() is the only
function in the file that both calls putfmt with arguments and takes
the address of a function, passing &order and &ex to sort().

Worth knowing: ccc reports this as

	Error: c1 failed on formatmw.c

with no indication that a pass dumped core.  A crash and a clean
diagnostic look the same from the driver.
